---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleCard from "../../components/ArticleCard.astro";

const articles = (await getCollection("articles"))
  .filter((article) => article.data.category === "Supply Chains")
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf());
---

<BaseLayout title="Food Supply Chains | Food Supply Crisis Report" description="Track shipping, ports, trade routes, freight, storage and logistics risks that can influence food availability and prices.">
  <section class="hero"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Topic Hub</p><h1>Food Supply Chain</h1><p>Food moves through farms, ports, ships, warehouses, roads and retailers. We explain how disruptions travel through that system.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <h2>How a disruption travels from a route to a food price</h2>
      <p>A shipping headline is only the beginning of the analysis. To understand the food impact, it is necessary to identify the affected cargo, route, timing and alternative capacity, then check how those changes affect landed costs and availability.</p>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card"><h3>1. Physical flow</h3><p>Which port, strait, railway, road or warehouse is affected, and what share of the relevant commodity normally uses it?</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>2. Alternative capacity</h3><p>Can cargo be rerouted or supplied from another exporter? Alternatives may take time, cost more or lack enough capacity.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>3. Delivered cost</h3><p>Freight, insurance, fuel, delays and financing affect the price paid by importers. The impact varies by contract and destination.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>4. Domestic transmission</h3><p>Currency, local inventory, harvests, taxes and competition influence whether an international shock reaches shops.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p>Do not infer a nationwide food shortage from a route disruption alone. Look for corroborating changes in trade flows, commodity prices, port operations and local availability. FAO's <a href="https://www.fao.org/worldfoodsituation/csdb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">cereal outlook</a> and market-specific official data help establish whether a logistics event is changing actual supply and trade.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="card-grid">
    {articles.length ? articles.map((article) => (<article class="card"><h2><a href={"/articles/" + article.id + "/"}>{article.data.title}</a></h2><p>{article.data.description}</p></article>)) : <div class="callout"><strong>Supply-chain coverage:</strong> Current reporting will connect shipping disruptions, freight costs, trade routes, ports and food-price effects.</div>}
  </div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
